\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Thanks for laying out the LeaderLens framework so clearly \'97 that context makes it possible to interpret your results responsibly. A quick visual of your seven scores first, then the full breakdown you asked for.A note on reading that chart: for the first four bars (dark tetrad signals), lower is generally more comfortable; for the last three (empathy, humility, ethical leadership), higher is generally more comfortable. So this isn't a "some bars good, some bad" chart \'97 it's two different scales sitting side by side. (Your study code is noted for your own records \'97 no action needed from me on it.)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 1. Balanced interpretation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Nothing here rises to a personality "type" \'97 these are self-reported tendencies, and your profile is genuinely mixed, which is the most common pattern LeaderLens is designed to surface.\
The shape worth paying attention to: 
\f1\b Psychopathy (68, elevated)
\f0\b0  and 
\f1\b Machiavellianism (64, moderate, near the elevated boundary)
\f0\b0  are your two highest dark-signal scores, while 
\f1\b Empathy (61)
\f0\b0  sits in "emerging" rather than "strength." Read together, this suggests a possible pattern of 
\f2\i moving fast and thinking strategically, without always pausing to check emotional or downstream impact first
\f0\i0  \'97 more likely to show up under time pressure, ambiguity, or competitive stakes than in calm, low-stakes moments.\
At the same time, 
\f1\b Humility (75)
\f0\b0  and 
\f1\b Ethical Leadership (68)
\f0\b0  are both reported strengths. This matters a lot: humility especially tends to function as a self-correcting mechanism \'97 it's associated with being more open to feedback, more willing to admit when a call was wrong, and less likely to double down defensively. That's a meaningful counterweight to the psychopathy/Machiavellianism signals, because unmanaged versions of those traits tend to get worse specifically when someone 
\f2\i isn't
\f0\i0  open to correction. You appear to be.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Likely triggers/context-sensitive risk points
\f0\b0  (worth treating as hypotheses, not facts):\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 High-pressure or deadline-compressed decisions, where there's less time for a consequence-check\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Competitive or political dynamics (resourcing, credit, cross-team turf) where strategic communication is tempting\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Moments of receiving pointed criticism or public correction\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Situations where you have more power/information than the other person\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Possible blind spot
\f0\b0 : because empathy is "emerging" rather than a strength, the risk isn't cruelty \'97 sadism is only moderate (43) \'97 it's more likely under-attending to how a decision 
\f2\i landed
\f0\i0  until after the fact. Bluntness or a fast call that was technically fine but interpersonally rough is a more probable failure mode than anything deliberately harmful.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 2. Top 3 behavioural risks to monitor\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Framed as patterns to watch, not identity labels:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 a) Skipping the consequence-check under time pressure.
\f0\b0  
\f2\i (linked to elevated Psychopathy signal)
\f0\i0  Observable version: making a call, sending the message, or greenlighting an action quickly, then only afterward realizing who it affects or how it will read. Watch for: decisions you made in under a minute that involved other people's work, reputation, or workload.\

\f1\b b) Selective or audience-tailored information sharing.
\f0\b0  
\f2\i (linked to moderate-high Machiavellianism signal)
\f0\i0  Observable version: giving stakeholder A a different emphasis or omission than stakeholder B on the same issue, in a way that serves your position rather than shared understanding. Watch for: catching yourself thinking "I'll frame it differently for them" in a way that isn't just tailoring communication style but changing the substance.\

\f1\b c) Bluntness that outpaces impact-checking.
\f0\b0  
\f2\i (linked to Empathy still being "emerging" rather than a strength, combined with the psychopathy signal's tendency toward reduced pausing)
\f0\i0  Observable version: feedback, decisions, or humour that land harder than intended, discovered only when someone reacts, goes quiet, or you hear about it secondhand.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 3. Top 3 strengths / protective capacities\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 a) Humility (75, strength).
\f0\b0  This is your biggest asset. It's the trait most associated with staying correctable \'97 inviting dissent, admitting mistakes, giving credit. It directly offsets both narcissistic self-protection and psychopathic overconfidence, because it keeps a feedback loop open.\

\f1\b b) Ethical Leadership (68, strength).
\f0\b0  Your reported instinct toward fairness and transparency is a direct counterweight to the Machiavellianism signal \'97 it's harder for selective information-sharing to take root when your default orientation is toward transparent process.\

\f1\b c) Empathy (61, emerging \'97 a real foundation, not a weakness).
\f0\b0  It's not yet a reported strength, but it's comfortably above the midpoint, meaning the raw capacity for perspective-taking is there. It's the most trainable of the three, and improving it does double duty: it's your best lever against both the bluntness risk and the "moving fast without checking impact" risk from the psychopathy signal.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 4. 2\'964 week micro-experiment plan\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Week 1 \'97 Build the pause.
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\i \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Micro-experiment
\f0\i0 : Before sending any decision, message, or feedback that affects someone else, insert one deliberate pause \'97 literally count to ten or wait until your next scheduled break \'97 and ask yourself "who is affected, and would I want to see this land the way I think it will?"\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\i \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Observable behaviour
\f0\i0 : Fewer same-minute reactive sends; you can point to at least 2\'963 instances where the pause changed your wording or timing.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\i \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reflection questions
\f0\i0 : When did I skip the pause this week, and what was the pressure that made me skip it? What did the pause change, if anything?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

